  "report": "The core architectural differences between Raft and Paxos lie in their leadership models and election mechanisms. Raft relies on a leader elected via randomized timeouts to serialize writes, ensuring all client requests go through that single leader of a term [1]. In contrast, basic Paxos uses proposers and acceptors without a dedicated leader, allowing any node to act as a proposer [2]. However, Multi-Paxos introduces a stable leader by electing a distinguished proposer to optimize performance [2]. A key distinction is that Raft requires log consistency before a node can become a leader, whereas basic Paxos does not enforce this requirement [2]. Despite these differences, both protocols use majority quorums to stay safe during partitions [1]. In terms of complexity and maintainability, Raft is considered simpler and more intuitive than Paxos because it was designed for understandability [3]. The original Paxos specification was notoriously ambiguous, leading to years of debate regarding its implementation [3]. Consequently, Raft's explicit state machine model leads to fewer implementation bugs as clearly specified states reduce errors [3]. Basic Paxos is also noted for having high implementation complexity in practice [4]. When choosing between them, Raft is preferable where developer productivity and maintainability matter most [4]. Conversely, Multi-Paxos variants are often compared against Raft specifically for high-throughput systems [4]. Ultimately, the choice depends on whether a team values clarity or theoretical flexibility [4]."
